------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
List = Shopping cart (add/remove anytime)
Tuple = Sealed box (fixed forever)
Both are used heavily in every DevOps script! 🚀
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔄 List vs Tuple – Full Comparison

Feature        List                 Tuple
--------------------------------------------------------
Syntax         [ ]                  ( )

Changeable     ✅ Yes               ❌ No

Add items      ✅ Yes               ❌ No

Remove items   ✅ Yes               ❌ No

Change items   ✅ Yes               ❌ No

Faster         ❌ Slower            ✅ Faster

Use for        Dynamic data         Fixed data

Example        List of servers      DB config

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
List and Tuple are built-in Python data types, not modules.

Category                Example
---------------------------------------------
Data Types              list, tuple, dict, set
Modules/Libraries       os, sys, requests, boto3

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
📋 LIST – Changeable Collection
Think of List as a Shopping Cart 🛒

You can ADD items to cart
You can REMOVE items from cart
You can CHANGE items in cart

# List uses [ ] square brackets
servers = ['web1', 'web2', 'web3']
List Operations

servers = ['web1', 'web2', 'web3']
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Access items (starts from 0)
print(servers[0])    # web1 (first)
print(servers[1])    # web2 (second)
print(servers[2])    # web3 (third)
print(servers[-1])   # web3 (last)

# 2. Add items
servers.append('web4')          # Add at end
servers.insert(0, 'web0')       # Add at position
print(servers)
# ['web0', 'web1', 'web2', 'web3', 'web4']

# 3. Remove items
servers.remove('web1')          # Remove by value
servers.pop()                   # Remove last item
servers.pop(0)                  # Remove by position
print(servers)

# 4. Change items
servers[0] = 'newserver'        # Change first item
print(servers)

# 5. Length
print(len(servers))             # How many items

# 6. Check if exists
if 'web2' in servers:
    print("web2 exists!")

# 7. Loop through
for server in servers:
    print(f"Server: {server}")

# 8. Sort
servers.sort()                  # Alphabetical
servers.sort(reverse=True)      # Reverse order

# 9. Clear
servers.clear()                 # Empty the list
